.TH HSH 1 "December 2024" "Simple Shell v1.0"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple shell, a basic UNIX command line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command line interpreter that replicates basic behavior of
the standard
.B /bin/sh
shell. It reads commands from standard input, parses them, searches for
executables in the PATH environment variable, and executes them using
system calls.
.PP
The shell supports both interactive and non-interactive modes.
.SH OPTIONS
The shell does not accept command-line options. All configuration is done
through environment variables and built-in commands.
.SH OVERVIEW
The shell operates in a continuous loop:
.IP 1. 3
Displays a prompt when in interactive mode
.IP 2. 3
Reads a command line from standard input
.IP 3. 3
Parses the command and arguments
.IP 4. 3
Executes built-in commands or searches for external commands
.IP 5. 3
Waits for command completion and displays next prompt
.PP
The shell terminates when it encounters the
.B exit
command or end-of-file (Ctrl+D).
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B main()
Entry point of the shell. Contains the main loop that reads, parses, and executes commands.
.TP
.B display_prompt()
Displays the shell prompt "#cisfun$ " when in interactive mode.
.TP
.B read_line()
Reads a line of input from standard input using getline().
.TP
.B split_line()
Splits the input line into tokens, handling whitespace separation.
.TP
.B trim_spaces()
Removes leading and trailing whitespace from input strings.
.TP
.B execute_command()
Executes commands by forking a child process and using execve().
.TP
.B find_in_path()
Searches for executable commands in the PATH environment variable.
.TP
.B _getenv()
Custom implementation of getenv() to retrieve environment variables.
.TP
.B print_env()
Prints all current environment variables (built-in env command).
.SH LIBRARY
The shell uses the following standard C libraries:
.IP \(bu 3
stdio.h - Standard input/output functions
.IP \(bu 3
stdlib.h - Memory allocation and process control
.IP \(bu 3
string.h - String manipulation functions
.IP \(bu 3
unistd.h - POSIX operating system API
.IP \(bu 3
sys/wait.h - Process waiting functions
.IP \(bu 3
sys/stat.h - File status functions
.IP \(bu 3
signal.h - Signal handling
.SH PATH SEARCH
When a command is entered without an absolute or relative path,
.B hsh
searches for the executable in the directories listed in the PATH
environment variable, in order. The search stops at the first matching
executable file. If PATH is unset or empty, the shell only executes
commands specified with an absolute or relative path.
.PP
The search algorithm:
.IP 1. 3
Check if command contains '/' character
.IP 2. 3
If yes, use as absolute/relative path directly
.IP 3. 3
If no, get PATH environment variable
.IP 4. 3
Tokenize PATH by ':' delimiter
.IP 5. 3
For each directory, construct full path: directory + "/" + command
.IP 6. 3
Check if file exists and is executable using stat()
.IP 7. 3
Return first valid path found
.SH WHITESPACE HANDLING
The shell handles whitespace as follows:
.IP \(bu 3
Leading and trailing whitespace is removed
.IP \(bu 3
Multiple spaces between arguments are treated as single delimiters
.IP \(bu 3
Empty lines (containing only whitespace) are ignored
.IP \(bu 3
The following whitespace characters are recognized: space, tab, newline, carriage return, form feed, vertical tab
.PP
Tokenization splits input on any whitespace character, preserving
quoted strings as single tokens (if implemented).
.SH BUILTINS
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell. Optional numeric argument sets exit status.
Syntax: exit [n]
.TP
.B env
Prints all current environment variables.
Syntax: env
.SH EXIT STATUS
The shell returns the following exit statuses:
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
General error (fork failure, etc.)
.TP
.B 2
Invalid argument to built-in command
.TP
.B 127
Command not found
.TP
.B Other
Exit status propagated from executed command
.PP
The exit status of the last executed command is available in the
.B $?
variable (if implemented).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The shell uses the following environment variables:
.TP
.B PATH
Colon-separated list of directories to search for executable commands.
If PATH is empty or not set, only absolute paths and relative paths
starting with "./" or "../" can be executed.
.TP
.B environ
Global variable containing the process environment.
.SH SIGNALS
.B hsh
ignores SIGINT (Ctrl+C) in the parent process to prevent accidental
termination of the shell itself. Child processes receive signals normally.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start an interactive session:
.RS
.PP
.nf
$ ./hsh
#cisfun$ /bin/ls
#cisfun$ ./myprogram
#cisfun$ exit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Execute multiple commands from a file:
.RS
.PP
.nf
$ cat commands.txt
ls -la
pwd
whoami
$ cat commands.txt | ./hsh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Path search example:
.RS
.PP
.nf
$ PATH="/bin:/usr/bin"
$ ./hsh
#cisfun$ ls      # Found in /bin/ls
#cisfun$ bash    # Found in /bin/bash
.fi
.RE
.SH RETURN VALUES
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 127
Command not found
.TP
.B 1
Fork error or other internal error
.TP
.B Other
Exit status of executed command
